## Test Anti-Pattern Audit: `SerializerTests.cs`

**Summary: 4 Critical · 1 High · 1 Medium · 0 Low**

This suite is dominated by **self-referential / tautological assertions** — 4 of 8 tests assert `output == input` on operations fed inputs already in their target state, making it impossible for the assertion to fail. Coverage numbers look fine because the production code runs, but these tests prove nothing.

---

### 🔴 Critical — Tests giving false confidence

#### 1. `UpperCase_ToUpper_Works` (line 72) — Tautological assertion

Input `"HELLO"` is already uppercase. `"HELLO".ToUpper()` always returns `"HELLO"`, so the assertion can never fail.

```csharp
// BEFORE — always passes, tests nothing
var input = "HELLO";
var result = input.ToUpper();
Assert.AreEqual(input, result);

// AFTER — actually tests the transformation
var result = "hello".ToUpper();
Assert.AreEqual("HELLO", result);
```

#### 2. `NormalizeName_AlreadyNormalized_NoChange` (line 37) — Tautological assertion

`Normalize()` does `Trim().ToLowerInvariant()`. Input `"alice"` is already trimmed and lowercase — the test only exercises the identity case and will never fail.

```csharp
// BEFORE — input is already in normalized form
var name = "alice";
var result = NameNormalizer.Normalize(name);
Assert.AreEqual(name, result);

// AFTER — verify actual normalization behavior
Assert.AreEqual("alice", NameNormalizer.Normalize("  ALICE  "));
Assert.AreEqual("bob jones", NameNormalizer.Normalize("Bob Jones"));
```

#### 3. `GetDisplayName_ReturnsFormattedName` (line 56) — Self-referential assertion

`GetDisplayName()` returns `Name` (line 10 of production code). The test asserts `user.Name == user.GetDisplayName()` — it's asserting a field against itself through a trivial passthrough. The test name promises "formatted" but verifies nothing about formatting.

```csharp
// BEFORE — asserts a field against itself
Assert.AreEqual(user.Name, display);

// AFTER — assert against a literal expected value
var user = new UserDto { Name = "Alice", Age = 30 };
var display = user.GetDisplayName();
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", display);
// If GetDisplayName should actually format (e.g., "Alice (30)"),
// update both the implementation and this assertion.
```

#### 4. `MapToDto_MapBack_RoundTrip` (line 46) — Tautological round-trip on a lossy mapping

The mapper is **lossy**: `ToDto` drops `Id` and `Email`, hardcodes `Age = 0`; `ToEntity` drops `Age`, hardcodes `Id = 0` and `Email = ""`. A round-trip assertion (`entity → dto → entity == entity`) is meaningless on a lossy transform — it either silently passes on the surviving fields or fails on the dropped ones. This test doesn't verify that either direction maps correctly.

```csharp
// BEFORE — round-trip on lossy mapping, proves nothing useful
var backToEntity = Mapper.ToEntity(dto);
Assert.AreEqual(entity.Id, backToEntity.Id);       // 1 vs 0 — should FAIL
Assert.AreEqual(entity.Email, backToEntity.Email);  // "a@b.com" vs "" — should FAIL

// AFTER — test each mapping direction independently with literal expectations
[TestMethod]
public void ToDto_MapsFullNameToName()
{
    var entity = new UserEntity { Id = 1, FullName = "Alice Smith", Email = "a@b.com" };
    var dto = Mapper.ToDto(entity);
    Assert.AreEqual("Alice Smith", dto.Name);
    Assert.AreEqual(0, dto.Age);  // document that Age is not mapped
}

[TestMethod]
public void ToEntity_MapsNameToFullName()
{
    var dto = new UserDto { Name = "Alice Smith", Age = 30 };
    var entity = Mapper.ToEntity(dto);
    Assert.AreEqual("Alice Smith", entity.FullName);
    Assert.AreEqual(0, entity.Id);     // document that Id is not mapped
    Assert.AreEqual("", entity.Email); // document that Email is not mapped
}
```

> ⚠️ **Note:** Based on the production code, the existing assertions on `Id` and `Email` should actually *fail* (`1 ≠ 0`, `"a@b.com" ≠ ""`). If these tests are reported as passing, the suite may not be running at all — investigate your CI pipeline.

---

### 🟠 High

#### 5. `Validate_ValidInput_ReturnsSameInput` (line 64) — One-sided testing

The test only covers the happy path (valid email returns input). There is **no test** for the rejection path — `ValidateEmail` throws `ArgumentException` on invalid input, but that behavior is completely unverified.

```csharp
// ADD — verify the rejection behavior
[TestMethod]
public void ValidateEmail_InvalidInput_ThrowsArgumentException()
{
    Assert.ThrowsException<ArgumentException>(
        () => Validator.ValidateEmail("not-an-email"));
}

[TestMethod]
public void ValidateEmail_EmptyInput_ThrowsArgumentException()
{
    Assert.ThrowsException<ArgumentException>(
        () => Validator.ValidateEmail(""));
}
```

---

### 🟡 Medium

#### 6. Missing parametrization opportunity (`NormalizeName`, `UpperCase`)

Once the Critical fixes above are applied, the normalize/uppercase tests are good candidates for `[DataRow]` parametrization to cover multiple inputs concisely.

---

